Must have

1. Auth and tenant setup

Login (JWT or simple email/password) and a tenant/project switcher.
Environment tabs: dev / staging / prod. Flags are scoped per environment.
API key page: generate and revoke SDK keys per environment (show the key once, store only the hash).

2. Flag list

Table with flag key, on/off toggle, environment, last updated, and who changed it.
Search and filter by tag or status.
Quick toggle with a confirm dialog on prod.

3. Flag detail / editor (the main screen)

Basic info: key, description, type (boolean, string, number, JSON).
Rules builder: add rows like attribute | operator | value → serve X, with drag to reorder since rule order matters.
Percentage rollout slider with a variant split editor (A 50% / B 50%, must sum to 100).
Default value and “off” value.
Save creates a new version. Handle the 409 conflict case: if someone else edited, show “flag changed, reload and retry”. This shows off your optimistic locking.

4. Version history and audit log

Timeline of versions per flag with a diff view (old vs new JSON).
Rollback to version N button, which creates a new version and doesn’t delete anything.
Global audit log page with filters by user, flag and date.

5. Flag tester (playground)

Input a sample user context (user_id, country, plan) and see which value is served and which rule matched.
This is the best demo feature. It calls your evaluation engine through an API, and interviewers can try it live.
Nice to have
Live updates: an SSE connection so the dashboard updates when another admin changes a flag. It also demonstrates your propagation layer visually.
Connected SDK instances: a list of clients with their last sync time and flag version, so you can see stale clients.
Exposure/metrics view: evaluation counts per variant as simple charts. Don’t build statistical analysis.
Environment compare: diff of flags between staging and prod.
Skip
Fancy analytics dashboards, user management with roles beyond admin/viewer, billing pages. They eat time and add nothing to the story.